bundle-doctor(1) -- Checks the bundle for common problems
=========================================================

## SYNOPSIS

`bundle doctor [diagnose]` [--quiet]
                           [--gemfile=GEMFILE]
                           [--ssl]<br>
`bundle doctor ssl` [--host=HOST]
                    [--tls-version=TLS-VERSION]
                    [--verify-mode=VERIFY-MODE]<br>
`bundle doctor cooldown` [--cooldown=NUMBER]<br>
`bundle doctor` help [COMMAND]

## DESCRIPTION

You can diagnose common Bundler problems with this command such as checking gem environment, SSL/TLS issue, or locked gems that are too new for the cooldown setting.

## SUB-COMMANDS

### diagnose (default command)

Checks your Gemfile and gem environment for common problems. If issues
are detected, Bundler prints them and exits status 1. Otherwise,
Bundler prints a success message and exits status 0.

Examples of common problems caught include:

* Invalid Bundler settings
* Mismatched Ruby versions
* Mismatched platforms
* Uninstalled gems
* Missing dependencies

**OPTIONS**

* `--quiet`:
  Only output warnings and errors.

* `--gemfile=GEMFILE`:
  The location of the Gemfile(5) which Bundler should use. This defaults
  to a Gemfile(5) in the current working directory. In general, Bundler
  will assume that the location of the Gemfile(5) is also the project's
  root and will try to find `Gemfile.lock` and `vendor/cache` relative
  to this location.

* `--ssl`:
  Diagnose common SSL problems when connecting to https://rubygems.org.

  This flag runs the `bundle doctor ssl` subcommand with default values
  underneath.

### ssl

If you've experienced issues related to SSL certificates and/or TLS versions while connecting
to https://rubygems.org, this command can help troubleshoot common problems.
The diagnostic will perform a few checks such as:

* Verify the Ruby OpenSSL version installed on your system.
* Check the OpenSSL library version used for compilation.
* Ensure CA certificates are correctly setup on your machine.
* Open a TLS connection and verify the outcome.

**OPTIONS**

* `--host=HOST`:
  Perform the diagnostic on HOST. Defaults to `rubygems.org`.

* `--tls-version=TLS-VERSION`:
  Specify the TLS version when opening the connection to HOST.

  Accepted values are: `1.1` or `1.2`.

* `--verify-mode=VERIFY-MODE`:
  Specify the TLS verify mode when opening the connection to HOST.
  Defaults to `SSL_VERIFY_PEER`.

  Accepted values are: `CLIENT_ONCE`, `FAIL_IF_NO_PEER_CERT`, `NONE`, `PEER`.

### cooldown

Checks the gems locked in the lockfile against the cooldown setting. The
cooldown setting only applies when Bundler resolves a new version, and a
version already in the lockfile is never excluded. A version that is too
new can still get into the lockfile, for example from a machine with no
cooldown configured or from an update made with `--cooldown 0`. Run this
command in CI to catch it.

Bundler fetches the publish time of each locked gem from its gem server.
It then compares the publish time with the cooldown that applies to the
source of the gem. If some locked gems are inside the cooldown window,
Bundler prints them with the number of days left and exits status 1.
Otherwise, Bundler prints a success message and exits status 0.

The check reads the lockfile as written, the same way a frozen install
does. It does not resolve the Gemfile again, and it never changes the
lockfile.

A locked version counts as inside the window when any published build
of that version is inside the window. This includes builds for
platforms that are not in the lockfile. The resolver applies the same
rule when it excludes a version.

Gems from git and path sources have no publish time, so the check skips
them. When the gem server does not give a publish time for a locked gem,
Bundler prints a warning that names the gem. That gem does not make the
check fail. See `cooldown` in bundle-config(1) for the gem servers that
give publish times.

When no cooldown applies to the sources of the locked gems, Bundler
says so and exits status 0 without a fetch from the gem servers.

**OPTIONS**

* `--cooldown=<number>`:
  Check against a cooldown of <number> days instead of the configured
  one. Pass `0` to disable the check for this run. See `cooldown` in
  bundle-config(1) for the precedence between the CLI flag, Bundler
  config, Gemfile per-source settings, and the gemrc file.
